B8, then I have B4x2, B5. Maybe that's better. \n\nAlternatively, discard T8, which is a single tile. \n\nThe safest tile to discard might be T8, since it's not part of any sequence others have. The next player has B8, but T8 is a different suit. \n\nAlternatively, discard W9, which is part of the 6-8-9 chow. If I discard W9, maybe someone needs it. But the previous player has a B6 pung, not W9. \n\nHmm. \n\nAnother angle: the scoring. If I can form a fully concealed hand (4 points), All Chows (2 points), and maybe other patterns. \n\nIf I discard a tile that doesn't break my potential. For example, if I discard T8, which isn't part of any chow, then I can keep the rest. \n\nBut I need to make sure that the remaining tiles can form the necessary melds. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard B4, I lose the chance to form a pung, but maybe it's better to discard it if I can't form it. \n\nBut with two B4s, maybe it's better to keep them. \n\nSo between the options, maybe discard T8. It's a single tile and not part of any sequence others have. \n\nSo the answer would be Play T8.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand has potential for a concealed hand with chows in Characters (1-2-3 and 6-8-9) and a pair. Discarding T8, a non-essential tile, preserves key sequences and avoids giving others needed tiles.]\nReason: [T8 is a single tile not part of any sequences others have, while keeping B4 pair allows potential pung formation. Discarding it maintains flexibility for All Chows or Half Flush scoring.]\nAnswer: Play T8"}
