if I discard B1, I can't form B1-B2-B3. \n\nSo, perhaps the best is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. \n\nLooking at my hand, W5, W8, W9, T4, J1 are not part of any potential melds. \n\nBetween these, W5, W8, W9 are suited tiles, but they are single. \n\nIf I discard W5, it's a middle tile. If I discard W9, it's a terminal. \n\nSince terminals are less useful for others, maybe discarding W9 is safer. \n\nAlternatively, discard J1, as it's an honor tile and might not help. \n\nBut I need to consider which discard gives me the best chance to improve. \n\nIf I discard W9, others might take it for a meld, but since it's a terminal, maybe not. \n\nIf I discard J1, it's an honor tile, which others might use for a Pung. \n\nBut since I only have one J1, and others might have none or one, it's possible. \n\nAlternatively, discard T1. It's a terminal, but maybe keeping it for All Terminals. \n\nConsidering that, maybe the best is to discard W5, which is a middle tile and not part of any potential meld. \n\nWait, but W5 could be part of a Pung if I get two more. But I have only one. \n\nAlternatively, discard W8. \n\nHmm. It's a bit tricky. \n\nAnother approach: look for which tile, if discarded, would leave me with the most flexibility. \n\nFor example, if I discard W1, I lose a potential pair but have one left. If I discard W1, the remaining W1 in my hand could be part of a pair. \n\nBut if I discard W1, I have one left, which could be the pair. \n\nAlternatively, if I keep W1, I have two, which is a pair. \n\nSo maybe it's better to keep W1 as a pair. \n\nThus, avoid discarding W1. \n\nLooking at the valid actions again, the options are: W5, W8, W9, W1, T4, B4, B7, T1, J1, B2, B1. \n\nIf I don't want to discard W1, then the options are the others. \n\nBetween the others, which is least useful. \n\nB1 and B2 are part of potential Chows. Discarding them would hinder. \n\nB4 is part of B3-B4-B5, but I don't have B3 or B5. \n\nB7 is a terminal. \n\nT1 is